1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,678
While the Directorate learned to govern a planet,
its Director was looking in the other

2
00:00:12,698 --> 00:00:13,242
direction.

3
00:00:13,811 --> 00:00:19,100
The BEYOND tab had four items in it on the first
night. He could afford none of them.

4
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:25,489
Eighty-four billion Points had bought a world. The
cheapest thing behind the seventh tab

5
00:00:25,509 --> 00:00:27,213
cost nine hundred billion.

6
00:00:27,783 --> 00:00:33,381
That was, he understood immediately, the answer to a
question he had been carrying for

7
00:00:33,401 --> 00:00:39,129
twenty-one years — why the System had ever bothered
with a dying laboratory in Delaware.

8
00:00:39,149 --> 00:00:43,832
It had not been building a businessman. It
had been raising the capital.

9
00:00:46,652 --> 00:00:47,768
Beyond.

10
00:00:56,987 --> 00:01:02,815
And beneath them, greyed out, without prices, eleven
more lines that he could not read

11
00:01:02,835 --> 00:01:03,291
at all.

12
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:11,829
What followed was the largest industrial mobilisation
in the history of the species, and

13
00:01:11,849 --> 00:01:13,734
it was not done with Points.

14
00:01:14,305 --> 00:01:20,675
He had, in twenty forty-seven, four hundred and
eighty thousand employees, a hundred and

15
00:01:20,695 --> 00:01:27,136
ninety million people in territories of association,
the executing authority of a hundred

16
00:01:27,156 --> 00:01:33,526
and forty-one governments, and the accumulated
manufacturing capacity of four of the six

17
00:01:33,546 --> 00:01:39,842
largest industrial groups on Earth. He also had,
from the moment the Accord was signed,

18
00:01:39,862 --> 00:01:46,231
the thing that no private enterprise had ever had
before: the ability to decide what the

19
00:01:46,251 --> 00:01:51,895
human race would build next, and to make that
decision stick for thirty years.

20
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:54,115
He decided on space.

21
00:01:54,685 --> 00:02:00,812
The public reason was true, and good, and had been
worked out by Tomás's department over

22
00:02:00,832 --> 00:02:07,029
four months so that every word of it would survive a
century of quotation: that a species

23
00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:13,456
with one planet is a species with one point of failure,
and that the Accord's entire purpose

24
00:02:13,476 --> 00:02:16,040
was the removal of points of failure.

25
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:21,873
The private reason was that he had seen eleven
greyed-out lines that he could not read.

26
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:29,954
The Directorate built launch capacity the way the
nineteen-forties had built ships. Not

27
00:02:29,974 --> 00:02:36,053
one vehicle at a time — an assembly line, in the
Atacama, and another in Queensland, and

28
00:02:36,073 --> 00:02:41,944
a third at Kourou, turning out a reusable
heavy-lift stage every nine days. By twenty

29
00:02:41,964 --> 00:02:48,043
fifty-one, Umbrella was putting more mass into orbit
every month than the human race had

30
00:02:48,063 --> 00:02:50,676
put up in its entire previous history.

31
00:02:52,746 --> 00:02:59,386
Dock One was assembled between twenty fifty-one and
twenty fifty-six, in a four-hundred-kilometre

32
00:02:59,406 --> 00:03:05,428
orbit, from four hundred thousand tonnes of material
that had been lifted from Earth and

33
00:03:05,448 --> 00:03:08,106
thirty-one million tonnes that had not.

34
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:14,336
The second number is the one that mattered, and it
is the reason the Belt came before

35
00:03:14,356 --> 00:03:19,013
the Moon. Umbrella did not go to space to
plant flags. It went to eat.

36
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:27,021
There is a thing about the space programme that the
Directorate's own historians tend

37
00:03:27,041 --> 00:03:32,734
to bury in an annex, and it deserves better, because
it is the closest Albert Wesker ever

38
00:03:32,754 --> 00:03:37,549
came to doing something for a reason he could
not justify on a spreadsheet.

39
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:39,927
He made it public. All of it.

40
00:03:40,497 --> 00:03:46,068
Every launch was broadcast. Every failure was
broadcast — and in the first four years

41
00:03:46,088 --> 00:03:51,791
there were nine, and four of them killed people, and
every single one was carried live,

42
00:03:51,811 --> 00:03:57,448
with the names released within the day, over the
objections of four separate bodies of

43
00:03:57,468 --> 00:04:03,236
the Directorate who pointed out, correctly, that no
state space programme in history had

44
00:04:03,256 --> 00:04:04,222
ever done this.

45
00:04:04,793 --> 00:04:10,553
The first was the Coronado in twenty fifty-two,
which broke up at forty kilometres with

46
00:04:10,573 --> 00:04:16,334
four crew aboard. The broadcast did not cut away.
The Directorate's public feed carried

47
00:04:16,354 --> 00:04:21,982
the telemetry, and the silence in the control room,
and then, nine minutes later, the

48
00:04:22,002 --> 00:04:27,630
flight director — a woman of thirty-four named
Priya Ramanathan, who had not slept in

49
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:33,277
two days — reading the four names into an open
microphone because nobody had told her

50
00:04:33,297 --> 00:04:33,742
not to.

51
00:04:34,312 --> 00:04:40,420
Four billion people watched it. It is the single
most-viewed piece of Directorate footage

52
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:43,380
that does not involve the
Director himself.

53
00:04:45,451 --> 00:04:51,379
He was asked about it once, years later, by an
engineer who had been in that control room,

54
00:04:51,399 --> 00:04:53,560
and who had the nerve to ask why.

55
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:59,206
Because in four hundred years there will be nine
billion human beings living off this

56
00:04:59,226 --> 00:05:04,534
planet, and every one of them will be there because
somebody's grandmother watched a rocket

57
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:09,686
fall apart on a screen in twenty fifty-two and put
her hand over her mouth and then, the

58
00:05:09,706 --> 00:05:12,555
following week, applied for the
programme anyway.

59
00:05:13,954 --> 00:05:19,342
You cannot build that with a press release. You can
only build it by letting them watch

60
00:05:19,362 --> 00:05:22,822
the real thing, including the parts
that are unbearable.

61
00:05:24,843 --> 00:05:30,585
The engineer, who had spent nineteen years assuming
the policy was a calculation, said

62
00:05:30,605 --> 00:05:34,605
that he had always wondered whether it
was for the families.

63
00:05:35,175 --> 00:05:40,919
The Director looked at him for a moment and said: it
was a calculation. And then he walked

64
00:05:40,939 --> 00:05:46,492
away, which the engineer recorded in his memoirs,
and which he said, forty years later,

65
00:05:46,512 --> 00:05:48,798
he had never been able to interpret.

66
00:05:51,867 --> 00:05:58,541
The first asteroid Umbrella captured was called 1993
UB, and it was six hundred metres

67
00:05:58,561 --> 00:06:05,156
across, and it contained more nickel and iron than
the human race had mined since the

68
00:06:05,176 --> 00:06:06,013
Bronze Age.

69
00:06:06,583 --> 00:06:12,353
By twenty fifty-nine there were four hundred and
eleven Umbrella vessels working the Belt,

70
00:06:12,373 --> 00:06:17,950
and the price of structural metal on Earth had
fallen by ninety-one percent, and eleven

71
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:23,933
national economies that had depended on mining had been
absorbed into association agreements,

72
00:06:23,953 --> 00:06:29,659
which was not an accident, and which Catherine
Adeyemi had modelled in twenty forty-eight

73
00:06:29,679 --> 00:06:34,098
and put in a memo that was read and
answered courteously and in full.

74
00:06:36,168 --> 00:06:42,105
Mars was settled in twenty sixty-one, because by then
it was cheap, and because a hundred

75
00:06:42,125 --> 00:06:47,862
and ninety million people in the territories of
association had been told for a decade

76
00:06:47,882 --> 00:06:53,551
that they were building a future off-world, and the
Directorate required that this be

77
00:06:53,571 --> 00:06:53,886
true.

78
00:06:54,456 --> 00:07:00,632
Nineteen thousand people went in the first wave.
Four hundred thousand had volunteered.

79
00:07:00,652 --> 00:07:06,687
The selection criteria were published in full, and
were scrupulously fair, and one of

80
00:07:06,707 --> 00:07:12,884
the eleven criteria was loyalty, and it was listed
fourth, and nobody objected, because

81
00:07:12,904 --> 00:07:16,303
by twenty sixty-one nobody
objected to anything.

82
00:07:18,872 --> 00:07:24,701
Evelyn Marsh moved her laboratory to orbit in twenty
fifty-eight. She was sixty-two years

83
00:07:24,721 --> 00:07:27,002
old and looked perhaps thirty-five.

84
00:07:27,572 --> 00:07:33,216
The Progenitor Initiative had eleven hundred staff
by then, and the great majority of

85
00:07:33,236 --> 00:07:39,414
them had never been human in the way that word is
usually meant. Its work had three branches.

86
00:07:39,434 --> 00:07:45,412
The first was the quiet ones, and there were now four
hundred of them, in positions across

87
00:07:45,432 --> 00:07:49,545
the Directorate, and none of them had
ever disobeyed anything.

88
00:07:50,115 --> 00:07:55,863
The second was the Tyrant line, which had moved on
considerably from a grey giant in a

89
00:07:55,883 --> 00:08:01,699
cradle. There were nine variants by twenty
fifty-eight. Four of them were not humanoid.

90
00:08:02,269 --> 00:08:07,887
The third branch had no name in any document, and
its purpose was to answer a question

91
00:08:07,907 --> 00:08:13,655
that Evelyn Marsh had first asked in twenty
twenty-eight, standing in front of a frosted

92
00:08:13,675 --> 00:08:19,358
window with her palm flat against the glass: what
happens to a human being who is given

93
00:08:19,378 --> 00:08:21,194
all of it, and does not die?

94
00:08:23,764 --> 00:08:29,763
She was the third branch. She had been for nineteen
years. She reported it to him every

95
00:08:29,783 --> 00:08:35,782
time, in memos of four lines, exactly as she had
promised in a white corridor in twenty

96
00:08:35,802 --> 00:08:36,544
thirty-six.

97
00:08:37,114 --> 00:08:40,361
He read every one. He never once
told her to stop.

98
00:08:42,931 --> 00:08:49,148
He bought the hull pattern in twenty sixty-six. Four
trillion Points, accumulated over

99
00:08:49,168 --> 00:08:52,630
nineteen years from the
dependency of a species.

100
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:59,159
The first void-cutter was two point eight
kilometres long, and it was not shaped like

101
00:08:59,179 --> 00:09:05,138
anything human beings had built before. Every ship
the species had ever made had been

102
00:09:05,158 --> 00:09:11,187
an argument with physics that ended in a compromise:
fuel here, crew there, radiators,

103
00:09:11,207 --> 00:09:17,307
symmetry. The pattern from the seventh tab did not
compromise. It was a long dark wedge

104
00:09:17,327 --> 00:09:23,286
with no windows and no markings and no visible
drive, and the engineers who assembled

105
00:09:23,306 --> 00:09:29,336
it — the human ones — found that they could follow
the instructions perfectly well and

106
00:09:29,356 --> 00:09:32,923
could not, afterwards, explain
what most of it did.

107
00:09:33,494 --> 00:09:35,147
It was called the Wilmington.

108
00:09:35,716 --> 00:09:41,863
Tomás's department had prepared a list of four hundred
names, drawn from a public competition

109
00:09:41,883 --> 00:09:47,632
that eleven million people had entered. The Director
read the list, and did not use it,

110
00:09:47,652 --> 00:09:53,467
and named the first ship of the human race's first
fleet after an industrial park on the

111
00:09:53,487 --> 00:09:59,568
south bank of a river in Delaware where six people had
once been unable to make the payroll.

112
00:10:00,138 --> 00:10:06,143
Nobody understood. It became, over the following
century, the most sacred word in the

113
00:10:06,163 --> 00:10:06,781
language.

114
00:10:09,351 --> 00:10:15,213
The signal arrived on the eleventh of March, twenty
seventy-one, at four in the morning,

115
00:10:15,233 --> 00:10:16,884
and it was not a message.

116
00:10:17,453 --> 00:10:23,330
That was the first thing the Directorate's
astronomers established, and it took them nine

117
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:29,028
days, and it was the only thing about it that was
ever comforting. It had no structure

118
00:10:29,048 --> 00:10:33,798
of the kind a message has. It did not repeat.
It was not aimed at Earth.

119
00:10:34,369 --> 00:10:40,570
It was a carrier — a continuous, extremely narrow,
extraordinarily powerful transmission

120
00:10:40,590 --> 00:10:46,579
at a frequency nobody had ever bothered to watch,
passing through the solar system on

121
00:10:46,599 --> 00:10:52,587
its way somewhere else, the way a searchlight
passes across a field without stopping.

122
00:10:53,158 --> 00:10:58,839
They traced its origin in four months. It came from
a direction thirty-one degrees off

123
00:10:58,859 --> 00:11:04,541
the plane of the ecliptic, and from very far away,
and from something that was moving.

124
00:11:05,111 --> 00:11:10,877
And in the ninth month, having listened to it
continuously, the Farside Array established

125
00:11:10,897 --> 00:11:13,998
the thing that ended the human
race's childhood.

126
00:11:14,567 --> 00:11:20,811
The carrier was modulated. Not with information —
with load. It flickered, very slightly,

127
00:11:20,831 --> 00:11:26,934
in a pattern that the array's engineers recognised
immediately because they had seen it

128
00:11:26,954 --> 00:11:29,609
a thousand times in their own systems.

129
00:11:30,178 --> 00:11:36,670
It was the signature of something drawing power. Enormous
amounts of it, in bursts, irregularly.

130
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:41,116
Whatever was out there was not
broadcasting. It was working.

131
00:11:44,686 --> 00:11:50,869
He read the report alone, in an observation deck on
Dock One, with the Earth going past

132
00:11:50,889 --> 00:11:51,654
underneath.

133
00:11:52,224 --> 00:11:58,458
He was eighty years old and looked thirty-five. He had
ruled the human species for twenty-four

134
00:11:58,478 --> 00:12:04,113
years, and had, in that time, done a very great
deal of good, which the narrator will

135
00:12:04,133 --> 00:12:08,038
not take away from him, and which does
not cancel anything.

136
00:12:08,609 --> 00:12:14,135
And he stood at the window and looked out at a sky
that had, four hours earlier, stopped

137
00:12:14,155 --> 00:12:14,892
being empty.

138
00:12:17,461 --> 00:12:20,659
There was nobody to say it to.
That was the thing.

139
00:12:21,230 --> 00:12:26,398
For twenty-one years there had been another voice in
that skull, and it would have had

140
00:12:26,418 --> 00:12:31,767
something to say about this — something historical,
and contemptuous, and probably wrong.

141
00:12:31,787 --> 00:12:36,955
It had been silent for twenty-four years. He had
stopped expecting it. He had stopped,

142
00:12:36,975 --> 00:12:42,083
mostly, remembering that it had ever been there, in
the way you stop hearing a clock.

143
00:12:42,653 --> 00:12:48,474
But standing at that window, looking at a sky with
something working in it, Albert Wesker

144
00:12:48,494 --> 00:12:53,264
found that he wanted, very much, to turn to
somebody and say: do you see?

145
00:12:55,835 --> 00:13:01,726
And he did turn. Physically. In an empty observation
deck, at four in the morning, three

146
00:13:01,746 --> 00:13:07,436
hundred kilometres above the Earth, an
eighty-year-old man in a black coat turned his

147
00:13:07,456 --> 00:13:12,206
head to speak to somebody who had not been
there for twenty-four years.

148
00:13:12,775 --> 00:13:14,097
Nobody answered.

149
00:13:17,918 --> 00:13:20,726
Beyond. Access expanded.

150
00:13:23,816 --> 00:13:25,718
The greyed-out lines resolved.

151
00:13:26,288 --> 00:13:32,085
He read them, standing at the window, with the Earth
turning below and the stars going

152
00:13:32,105 --> 00:13:38,104
past. And for the first time since a washroom in the
rain in September twenty twenty-six,

153
00:13:38,124 --> 00:13:42,162
Albert Wesker had absolutely no idea
what he was looking at.
